{
  "evidence": "TEXT_D: +1p on Income Tax to fund NHS and social care; extensive social security reforms (end two‑child limit/benefit cap, abolish bedroom tax, right to food, end fuel poverty by 2025); 300,000 homes/year incl. 100,000 for social rent; strong mental health guarantees; ending hostile environment with humane asylum (resettlement targets, 28‑day detention limit); protect HRA/ECHR; NATO 2% and maintain a minimum nuclear deterrent; £1bn for community policing; tougher anti‑avoidance, align capital gains with income tax, binding shareholder votes on executive pay. TEXT_G: NHS Protection Act and compensation for contaminated blood; end austerity and benefit freeze/sanctions with uprating; protect pension triple lock; treat drug deaths as a public health emergency; expanded parental leave and free higher education; crack down on tax avoidance/evasion; cancel the UK nuclear weapons system; seek a legally undisputed Scottish independence referendum; retain bases/regiments and improve veterans’ housing.",
  "reasoning": "Both texts strongly address Care and Equality, but TEXT_D offers broader, UK‑wide, concrete investments and protections while also advancing proportional‑fairness measures. Given the electorate’s relatively high Loyalty/Authority weights, TEXT_D’s commitment to NATO and a minimum nuclear deterrent aligns better than TEXT_G’s push for Scottish independence and scrapping the nuclear deterrent.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
